agent_message line=12 text=I’m using the Kit verification workflow because this task requires an implementation, K semantics integration, a full-domain proof, and the Gate A/B/C audit. I’ll first read the workflow and repository inputs, then follow its routing exactly and keep all generated artifacts inside `/workspace`.
agent_message line=22 text=The workflow routes this through live verification, not the paper fallback. I’m now loading the shared soundness contract—especially the exact Gate definitions—and checking which Python constructs the supplied semantics and translator actually support before choosing the implementation.
agent_message line=28 text=The task is a symbolic proof over an unbounded string domain, so I’m adding the Kit’s specification, proof-construction, invariant, and validation skills in that order. The likely proof shape is a recursive/loop summary over the supplied string semantics; I won’t extend operational semantics unless inspection exposes a genuine missing construct.
agent_message line=49 text=The supplied semantics has a direct, fixed-semantics implementation of `str.swapcase()` and defines it structurally as `mapSwap` over an arbitrary symbolic `IntSeq`. I’m choosing the one-line Python implementation so the proof can execute the real function body and real method dispatch, with no operational shortcut or custom result oracle.
agent_message line=64 text=The implementation translates cleanly to the expected AST constructor term. Next I’m compiling the untouched reference semantics with the mandated LLVM modules, then I’ll run both definition loading and actual calls—including the prompt example and edge cases—through `krun`.
agent_message line=93 text=Concrete execution passed the prompt example, empty input, punctuation/digits, and mixed case. The failed `chr(233)` run is useful model-boundary evidence: the supplied semantics explicitly refuses non-ASCII character construction, while CPython maps `é` to `É`. I’ll keep that as an expected boundary probe, not misreport it as a program failure.
agent_message line=116 text=The first symbolic run exposed only a claim-syntax error: one missing closing parenthesis around `#loadAll`, before any proof search began. I’m correcting that exact parse issue and rerunning the same fixed-semantics claim.
agent_message line=131 text=The full symbolic target claim already closes with `#Top` and exit 0 for an unconstrained `CS:IntSeq`, so it is not a bounded-length proof. I’m now applying the validation skill: rebuilding the extension inventory, then running an independent wrong-result probe and a body-mutation probe to confirm the theorem is both result-constraining and sensitive to the actual implementation.
agent_message line=161 text=Both negative proofs behaved correctly: the wrong postcondition reached `"a"` while demanding `"A"` and exited 1; the identity-body mutation reached `"A"` while demanding `"a"` and exited 1. Because `verification.k` contains no proof-local rules or summaries at all, there is no Gate A repair loop to trigger—the successful theorem comes entirely from executing the supplied semantics.
agent_message line=172 text=The audit is now `VALIDATED`: Gate A passes with no proof extensions, Gate B covers the unbounded reference-model string domain with the Unicode model boundary explicitly isolated, and Gate C has reproducible smoke, differential, vacuity, and body-sensitivity evidence. I’m replaying `prove.sh` end-to-end once more to ensure the checked-in commands—not just the interactive steps—produce the same statuses.
agent_message line=198 text=Implemented and proved the task. - [solution.py](/workspace/solution.py) and [solution.mpy](/workspace/solution.mpy) - [verification.k](/workspace/verification.k) and [spec.k](/workspace/spec.k) - [prove.sh](/workspace/prove.sh) replays the complete workflow successfully. - [PROOF.md](/workspace/PROOF.md) reports `VALIDATED`, including Gate A/B/C audits and the documented Unicode model boundary. - The unbounded `CS:IntSeq` target proof printed `#Top` and exited 0. - Wrong-result and body-mutation probes both correctly exited 1. RESULT: KPROVE_PASSED — The full-domain symbolic target proof printed #Top with exit 0 and passed Gate B under the supplied reference semantics.
